---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/alternatives/firepush-alternative";
const title = "Firepush alternatives: preserve push or rebuild legacy channels?";
const desc = "Choose around current web push needs and legacy email or SMS continuity. Keep working recovery while testing reach, permission, export and queue handoff.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I retain Firepush?",
    "answer": "Yes, when supported browser notifications meet the store\u2019s goals and the actual reach, purchase suppression and cost checks pass."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Firepush currently provide email and SMS?",
    "answer": "Its current comparison page explicitly describes web push only. Treat historical email/SMS records as a separate support and continuity investigation."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can push subscribers become email subscribers?",
    "answer": "Browser permission does not grant email or SMS permission. Establish the intended channel permission separately and preserve opt-outs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can push subscriptions transfer?",
    "answer": "Require confirmation of origin, service-worker and subscription-key compatibility and receiving-provider support, then test a controlled subscription."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Sequenzy a direct replacement?",
    "answer": "Require a demonstration of the required Shopify data and lifecycle behavior. An email candidate is not automatically a browser-push replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "When can I disconnect?",
    "answer": "After export reconciliation, queue cancellation, purchase and permission exits, reply ownership, billing and rollback checks pass for the actual dependencies."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8"><a class="link" href="/alternatives">Alternative guides</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Migration decisions</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p></header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>What Firepush does now, and what a legacy account may still need</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/">Firepush’s current homepage</a> offers Shopify web push for welcome, cart recovery, price-drop and back-in-stock messages, with subscriber growth tools and analytics. Its <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/web-push-apps-compared/">web push comparison</a> explicitly says Firepush sends web push only. Checked November 25, 2026. These are useful reasons to evaluate or retain the product when browser notifications meet the store’s needs.</p>
<p>Older email or SMS records require a separate continuity investigation. Do not infer that those channels can be activated today from an old app description, old invoice or saved template. Ask support what historical data can still be retrieved, through which export path, and until when. A current push replacement does not recover a legacy SMS number, email suppression list or inaccessible dashboard.</p>
<h2>Choose the job before the destination</h2>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Candidate direction</th><th>Proof to request</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Keep browser recovery working</td><td>Retain Firepush or evaluate a push provider.</td><td>Supported devices, prompt behavior, token portability, purchase exit and queue cancellation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coordinate push with other marketing</td><td>Evaluate Omnisend against the existing senders.</td><td>Actual channel eligibility, cross-channel exclusions and late purchase handling, not just a shared interface.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rebuild email lifecycle</td><td>Compare Shopify Messaging, Klaviyo, Mailchimp or an event-driven email candidate such as Sequenzy.</td><td>Required Shopify events, catalog fields, consent updates and operator editing exercise. No Shopify parity is assumed for Sequenzy.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continue legacy SMS</td><td>Evaluate a supported SMS service, including Postscript where appropriate.</td><td>Destination coverage, number ownership, transfer process, opt-outs, replies and carrier charges.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Change capture without changing the sender</td><td>Evaluate Privy separately.</td><td>Form destination, list mapping and channel-specific permission evidence.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>These are unranked evaluation paths. <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">Shopify documents Messaging, formerly Shopify Email</a>, with campaigns and email/SMS automations; compare that native baseline before adding a service. Check account and regional availability. <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">Yotpo’s notice says its SMS application is decommissioned</a>, including dashboard and background services. SMSBump is a legacy exit destination, not a new replacement recommendation.</p>
<h2>Inventory three different audiences</h2>
<p>Count browser subscriptions, email addresses and phone numbers separately. Push permission is not email or SMS permission; matching identifiers does not create it. Record source, timestamp, purpose, channel, withdrawal and latest preference state for each available record. Preserve suppression and deletion obligations alongside active subscribers. Quarantine unresolved conflicts for the responsible permission reviewer instead of letting an old import overwrite a newer opt-out.</p>
<p>Request a sample export before cancellation: customer and checkout IDs, subscription status, tags, event timestamps, automation definitions, campaign identifiers, delivery/click/order reporting and any legacy email/SMS consent evidence. Store an access-controlled original and a field dictionary with time zones and export date. Reconcile counts by status, not just total rows. If export is unavailable, document the gap and support ticket; do not substitute scraped tokens or treat missing consent as permission.</p>
<h2>Browser reach and portability exercise</h2>
<p>The current Firepush homepage describes desktop and Android browser support and says iPhone shoppers must add the store to the home screen before subscribing. Test the exact supported devices and prompt path with each receiving provider. Count eligible visits, granted subscriptions, deliveries and purchases separately. Total mobile traffic is not the push-addressable audience, and a product-specific restriction is not a universal iOS failure.</p>
<p>Ask both providers about service-worker scope, store origin, subscription keys and import support. An exported token file alone does not prove that a new sender can deliver. Test a controlled subscription without destroying the old working setup. If re-subscription is required, design a clear new prompt, avoid repeated prompts to declined users and budget the smaller reachable audience. Do not promise a full-list transfer.</p>
<h2>Proposed accessories-store acceptance fixtures</h2>
<p>These are exercises to run, not observed merchant outcomes. Use a case and cable with different stock states, a long product title, an expired offer and a sold-out variant. A price-drop notification must open the intended product; a back-in-stock message must not advertise an unavailable variant. Check closed-tab delivery and a revoked browser permission. Record screenshots, event IDs and actual behavior for each fixture.</p>
<p>For cart recovery, distinguish an anonymous cart, identified checkout and completed order. Replay a duplicate event with the same stable key; it should not create a second enrollment. Place an order after enrollment but before dispatch and require obsolete recovery to stop. Repeat with an opt-out and a delayed purchase webhook. If an identifier cannot be mapped safely, hold the message rather than merge shoppers by address alone.</p>
<h2>Queue handoff and rollback</h2>
<p>Make a ledger of every active trigger, delay, queue, sender and kill switch. Assign one owner per recovery purpose across push, email and SMS. Freeze edits for the handoff, inspect messages already scheduled in both systems, cancel or suppress the departing owner, then enable the accepted replacement. Recheck purchase, permission, eligibility and frequency at dispatch. A disabled workflow may leave queued messages behind; verify actual cancellation behavior.</p>
<p>Keep a reversible configuration snapshot and name the person authorized to pause the new sender. Rehearse a stale message and a missed purchase event before live expansion. On failure, pause the affected purpose, reconcile queued and already-sent IDs, restore only eligible work to the former owner if it remains supported, and avoid replaying messages already delivered. Define the observation period from the longest queue and event delay, not a universal two-week schedule.</p>
<h2>Dependencies, replies and support continuity</h2>
<p>Inventory theme snippets, checkout extensions, popups, webhooks, subscriptions, loyalty, reviews, helpdesk connections and reports. Record data direction, destination list or tag, credentials owner and downstream consumer. Test new capture and checkout paths before removing the old integration. Preserve UTM naming and attribution-window definitions so reports remain interpretable.</p>
<p>Push clicks lead to storefront destinations; they do not replace an email reply inbox or SMS conversation queue. For legacy channels, identify the reply address, phone number owner, open conversations and escalation team. Test a reply, an opt-out and a failed delivery with the replacement, including after-hours ownership. Ask the provider for a case-specific number-transfer plan and retain support correspondence. Do not schedule disconnection before unresolved support and record-access dependencies are accounted for.</p>
<h2>Cost and contribution without invented channel winners</h2>
<p>Request dated quotes for the same reachable audience and expected sends. Include push allowances and overages, retained email/capture services, SMS destination and carrier charges, support, implementation, operator time and overlap. Keep message counts separate from subscriber counts. Compare staying, fixing configuration, replacing push alone and consolidating; no combination is mandatory.</p>
<p>Illustrative arithmetic: 4,000 eligible subscriptions × 2 planned notifications = 8,000 planned sends before retries or exclusions. If a proposed change saves $150 monthly and costs $1,800 to implement, fee-only payback is 12 months. A hypothetical $300 of incremental contribution less $100 additional operating cost leaves $200, not $300. These are planning inputs, not Firepush prices or measured recovery results. If net savings are zero or negative there is no fee-only payback.</p>
<p>Compare contribution after discounts, returns, fulfillment and channel costs with the same eligibility and attribution windows. Deduplicate orders credited by multiple senders. A lower push bill does not prove higher incremental profit; a controlled comparison may justify keeping Firepush. Expand only after the receiving provider’s delivery, reputation and permission checks pass for any email/SMS work; a fixed ramp calendar is not evidence of readiness.</p>
<h2>Operational sign-off and retirement</h2>
<p>Name owners for copy, stock and discount rules, capture, suppression, billing, replies and incident response. Agree what an agency rebuilds, what internal staff approve and when contractor access expires. Train a second operator to pause and edit the accepted flows. Retain necessary records under the store’s retention policy, confirm billing cancellation and revoke unused credentials after evidence and rollback needs are resolved. Compare channel results and maintenance effort against the saved baseline without assuming a revenue lift.</p>
